 - [2026-09 — Rebuilding the orchestrator's host views every run instead of caching them per allocation](2026-09-hbg-per-run-host-view-rebuild.md) — **measured & dropped**: the per-allocation view cache #2205 prescribes is needed, and the reason is a term #1848 could not see. #1848 read `halHostRegister`/`halHostUnregister` as essentially all per-byte (~6.7 ms/GiB) by *inferring* from the size asymmetry of one aggregate point, and its own text says the per-call split was never measured. Sweeping size at fixed call count finds **a ~5.2 µs floor per register/unregister pair** that is flat from 64 B to 4 KiB, alongside a **7.0 ms/GiB** slope above ~1 MiB that does confirm #1848 for large buffers — both readings are right in their own regime. Register is per mapped *page*, and the page size is the allocation's, so 256 KiB on ordinary pages (64 pages, 11.34 µs) costs more than 4 MiB on huge pages (2 pages, 3.43 µs); that non-monotonicity is the mechanism, not noise, and it is #1531's page-configuration sensitivity again. A realistic `paged_attention` bind reads two control tensors for **24 µs/run, ~4% of a 0.6 ms warm `chip.run`**, against a decision rule of 10 µs fixed before the number was seen. Two details beyond the ratio: the floor means cost tracks the *number* of accessed tensors rather than their size, so a 32-byte control tensor still costs 5.2 µs; and the slope makes #2205's stated trigger a cliff — rebuild would charge 1.77 ms/run for a 256 MiB orchestrator-accessed tensor, three times the whole run. Caching also removes a crossover nobody wants to own as policy: below ~3 accesses, per-access `rtMemcpy` (~1.5 µs) beats establishing a mapping at all. The three arguments for rebuilding that hold regardless of cost — invalidation safety, the accessed set changing between runs, and pinned page-table entries — survive as design constraints on the cache rather than as reasons to skip it
 
 - [2026-09 — Widening the bounded WAIT-reduction bitmap past BL=64](2026-09-wait-reduction-bitmap-window-sizing.md) — **BL=64 shipped (#2009 / issue #1376), BL=128 and BL=256 deferred**, and the larger result: **BL=64 is end-to-end neutral on both production workloads** — but *amended 2026-09-07*, that is a property of those graphs, not of the mechanism: both are near-chains (per-task fanin degree ≤ 1.5 across the whole benchmark corpus, and two of its cases have **zero** redundant edges), so there is almost nothing for reduction to remove. Under a degree-16 graph (`sliding_window_deps`, 15,865 WAIT edges of which BL=64 removes 100%) the same code is **−20.47% `Orch` and −2.59% `Effective`**, and `Orch` *falls* rather than rises because `wire_fanin_task` is charged to that span — ~26 ns saved per removed edge against a per-submit bitmap cost an order of magnitude smaller. Widening only moves one of the two production workloads — Qwen3-14B decode removes 1 of 40 redundant edges at *every* window because 39 of them are cross-ring long edges outside BL=256 too, while DeepSeek-V4 FLASH decode goes 10,065 → 20,214 of 21,698 (2.0x) — against `WaitReachEntry` growing 16 → 40 B per slot (1 → 2.5 MiB) and a 1-word shift-merge becoming 4-word on the AICPU submit path for every task. But the same campaign shows the removals buy no time to begin with: 10 pinned-die rounds put every row inside the ±1.1% run-to-run band, which is the *expected* result because bounded reduction preserves WAIT reachability exactly, so no task's earliest start time moves. Instrumentation says why so little is left to make cheaper — **~90% of the 9,495 cleared edges point at producers already `CHIP_TASK_COMPLETED` at wiring**, which take the `completed_fanin` branch and never allocate a dep-pool entry, so the real saving is 990 of 19,114 entries (−5.2%) and −4.2% peak occupancy, not on the critical path of a ~30 ms AICore-bound step. Consequences worth carrying forward: the simulator's `estimated_dep_pool_entries_removed` is an **edge-count upper bound that overstates the runtime saving ~10x** (its *edge* counts are good — `drop` predicted exactly, total within 6%); and two DeepSeek-V4 aggregations produce wrong answers — keeping decode step 0 reports **−37.8%** from a single 7x launch-skew sample whose spike sits entirely in `Sched`, and the first step's *slow* rank cannot separate the arms at all (rank-sum U=5 against a critical value of 1), so only the fast rank is a clean first-step signal
